# Copy this file to .env.gmail (same folder) and fill in your own Google
# OAuth "Desktop app" client if you're building/distributing your own fork of
# this app. Once set, every end user just clicks "Connect Gmail" in Settings
# -> Email Integrations - no per-user Google Cloud project needed. Users can
# still override it with their own Client ID/Secret via the "Advanced" option
# in that screen (useful if they want their own 100-test-user quota, or to
# keep their data on an OAuth client they control).
#
# How to get these values (Google Cloud Console - console.cloud.google.com):
#   1. Create or select a project.
#   2. APIs & Services -> Library -> enable "Gmail API".
#   3. APIs & Services -> OAuth consent screen:
#        - User type: External.
#        - Scopes: add https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.readonly
#        - While in "Testing" mode, add up to 100 test-user emails manually -
#          no review needed, good enough for early users.
#        - To allow arbitrary/public users beyond that 100 cap, submit for
#          Google's verification review and move the app to "In production".
#          gmail.readonly is a "restricted" scope, so verification includes a
#          security assessment - budget real time for this, it's a Google
#          process outside this app's control.
#   4. APIs & Services -> Credentials -> Create Credentials -> OAuth client ID
#        - Application type: "Desktop app" (NOT "Web application" - Desktop
#          app clients support the loopback-redirect flow this app uses, and
#          Google's own docs say a Desktop app's client secret isn't treated
#          as confidential, since an installed app can't keep it secret
#          anyway - so baking one shared client into the app is an accepted,
#          standard pattern, not a security downgrade).
#   5. Copy the generated Client ID and Client Secret below.
GMAIL_DEFAULT_CLIENT_ID=
GMAIL_DEFAULT_CLIENT_SECRET=
GMAIL_DEFAULT_REDIRECT_URI=http://127.0.0.1:8001/api/email/oauth/callback
